  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nMostly clear skies and light to calm wind will allow for favorable \nconditions for fog overnight Monday night which the REFS is quite \ninterested in. With surface temperatures hanging just above freezing \nbut dew points around or just below freezing, some of this \ncould be freezing fog. Think it will be primarily within \nvalleys, but some patches otherwise are possible as well. Fog \nwill dissipate after sunrise as temperatures begin to rise.\n\nDry weather returns across the board through the upcoming week as \nmid-level ridging builds across the eastern half of the country and \nexpansive surface high pressure does the same. The column turns bone \ndry with ensemble mean PWATs a quarter inch or less. This will allow \nfor diurnal mixing to erode a few degrees off of dew points in the \nafternoons. Temperatures will gradually moderate as well back into \nthe 60s for most of the area by the end of the week when the surface \nhigh that initially sits overhead slides east and promotes southerly \ngradient flow. Probability for >60 degree highs reaches only\naround 20% on Wednesday but then jumps to 60-90% come Thursday \nand Friday. We will see some decent diurnal temperature swings \nas light wind and clearing skies allow for efficient radiational\ncooling and a good probability of a couple consecutive nights \nof lows in the 20s during the first half of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOur next chance for rain looks far off tied to a possible \ntrough swinging through the central CONUS and then Ohio Valley \nnear the end of the upcoming weekend. Depth of the trough varies\nwith a deeper one introducing higher rain chances and a quicker,\nshallower one with lower rain chances. Some added uncertainty \narises from possible development of a coastal low and how far \ninland it's able to work, though consensus for now keeps it off\nto our east. Ensemble sensitivity analysis suggests we get a \nclearer picture of the synoptic evolution on Friday with \nanalysis of two features: 1) the parent trough ejecting out of \nthe Rockies and 2) a separate wave sliding down through \nManitoba. A stronger embedded shortwave on the leading edge of \nthe ejecting trough would lend to faster longwave movement and \nlesser rain chance locally. A stronger embedded shortwave in \nManitoba could then phase with the parent CONUS trough and lend \nto higher rain chance locally.",
